| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | Undefined | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |

For example, at $x=2$, $y=-\dfrac{4}{2}=-2$. At $x=0$, division by zero is undefined.

Since $k=-4<0$, the branches lie in quadrants $2$ and $4$. The asymptotes are $x=0$ and $y=0$; there are no intercepts.

As $x$ becomes very large positively, $y$ approaches $0$ from below. As $x$ becomes very large negatively, $y$ approaches $0$ from above.

Plot the table points and draw two smooth branches approaching the axes.

